const CENTURIES = [
  {"n": 1, "roman": "I", "years": "1 – 100 CE", "regime": "property", "pop": [450000, 1000000], "cluster": 0.15, "walls": "servian", "spill": 0.25, "popNote": "The classic figure is about a million, argued from the grain dole (Augustus records over 200,000 male recipients in 2 BCE) and from the insula counts in the later regionary catalogues. Storey put it near 450,000 instead, on the grounds that a million inside the walls means Hong Kong density. Nobody counted the city. The range is the honest answer.", "free": 6, "band": "months", "title": "A man who cannot name his people", "body": ["The largest city on earth, and a large share of its labour is owned outright. You arrive filthy, speaking nothing anyone recognises, unable to name a father, a tribe, a patron or a home town, wearing clothes of a cut no one has seen and carrying a knife you cannot explain.", "Roman law had no gentle reading of that. Free status was a thing you proved, through witnesses, a patron, a birth registration. Someone held as a slave carried the burden of proving otherwise, and in classical law could not even plead their own freedom suit: a free third party, the <i>adsertor libertatis</i>, had to stand up and do it for them. You do not know one person in this city."], "danger": "Recovery of runaways was a profession. The <i>fugitivarius</i> worked ports and markets for the reward, and magistrates were required to detain anyone suspected of being a <i>fugitivus</i>.", "ends": "Not death. A sale."},
  {"n": 2, "roman": "II", "years": "101 – 200 CE", "regime": "property", "pop": [450000, 1000000], "cluster": 0.15, "walls": "servian", "spill": 0.25, "popNote": "The peak, wherever the peak actually was. Same dispute as the 1st century and no better evidence: the figure is inferred from grain and buildings, never observed.", "free": 6, "band": "months", "title": "The peak, which is the worst possible arrival", "body": ["Everything works. Eleven aqueducts running, a grain fleet from Egypt and Africa, free baths, free games, a standard of urban living Europe will not see again for seventeen hundred years.", "None of it is addressed to you. Each of those systems is keyed to a status you cannot demonstrate: the grain dole went to named men on a list. Around forty-five inscribed slave collars survive from the later empire, and one of them, the Zoninus collar, still carries its tag: <i>Fugi, tene me. Cum revocaveris me domino meo Zonino, accipis solidum.</i> I have run away, hold me; return me to my master Zoninus and you get a gold coin. That is the object the city has ready for a man who cannot account for himself."], "danger": "The Antonine Plague, 165–180, which you are immune to, and the panic around it, which you are not. Cassius Dio reports 2,000 dying a day in Rome in the 189 outbreak.", "ends": "Not death. A sale."},
  {"n": 3, "roman": "III", "years": "201 – 300 CE", "regime": "property", "pop": [400000, 900000], "cluster": 0.2, "walls": "aurelian", "spill": 0.1, "popNote": "Falling, from an unknown starting point, after the Plague of Cyprian (249–262). The decline is a shape in the evidence, not a series of figures.", "free": 8, "band": "months", "title": "The century the city admits it is afraid", "body": ["Civil wars, coups, a currency that stops meaning anything, and in 271 Aurelian starts building a wall around a city that had not needed one for five hundred years. It is finished around 275: roughly nineteen kilometres of circuit, enclosing about 13.7 square kilometres, brick-faced concrete, eight metres high before Maxentius and Honorius doubled it.", "It is still there. Those walls remained Rome’s actual military defence until Italian artillery breached them at the Porta Pia on 20 September 1870, roughly sixteen hundred years in service. Everything that follows on this page happens inside them."], "danger": "Debasement wrecks the price of everything. A man whose only asset is his own labour is standing in a market where labour itself is the commodity being traded.", "ends": "Not death. A sale."},
  {"n": 4, "roman": "IV", "years": "301 – 400 CE", "regime": "property", "pop": [500000, 800000], "cluster": 0.2, "walls": "aurelian", "spill": 0.08, "popNote": "The regionary catalogues, which list some 46,000 insulae, date from this century. They are the main evidence for the imperial millions and their interpretation is the whole argument.", "free": 10, "band": "months", "title": "Charity is invented, and so is deportation", "body": ["Christianity becomes legal, then favoured, and brings an organised duty of care toward strangers as such. The <i>xenodochium</i>, a house that receives foreigners, appears as a real institution: Pammachius and Fabiola founded one at Portus, Rome’s harbour, in the 390s.", "The same century shows the other face. Ammianus Marcellinus records, with disgust, that when grain ran short Rome expelled the <i>peregrini</i>, the foreigners, from the city wholesale, while keeping the dancers and dismissing the teachers. This is the first century in which the city has both instincts at once: a bed for the stranger, and a mechanism for removing him."], "danger": "Famine expulsions. The first thing a food shortage does to a city is redefine who counts as belonging to it.", "ends": "A sale, or the road, depending on the harvest."},
  {"n": 5, "roman": "V", "years": "401 – 500 CE", "regime": "property", "pop": [100000, 400000], "cluster": 0.25, "walls": "aurelian", "spill": 0.05, "popNote": "A steep fall across the century, all of it modern estimate. Something around 100,000 by 500 under Theodoric is the common guess.", "free": 10, "band": "months", "title": "Sacked twice, and still the largest thing anyone has seen", "body": ["Alaric takes the city in 410 after three sieges; the Vandals take it over two weeks in 455. Neither ends Rome. What drains it is slower and duller: the court had already moved to Ravenna, and when the Vandals took Carthage in 439 the African grain that made a million people possible in a place that could not feed them stopped arriving.", "The city is not destroyed. It is defunded. People leave because the free bread that was the entire economic basis of living here has gone."], "danger": "The sieges rather than the sacks. Zosimus reports rumours of cannibalism inside the walls in 409. Both sacks took captives, and captives were sold.", "ends": "Starvation, or a slave ship."},
  {"n": 6, "roman": "VI", "years": "501 – 600 CE", "regime": "turning", "pop": [30000, 100000], "cluster": 0.3, "walls": "aurelian", "spill": 0.02, "popNote": "The sharpest collapse in the city’s history: perhaps 100,000 early in the century, around 30,000 by the 550s. Both ends are reconstructions.", "free": 1, "band": "weeks", "title": "The worst century to arrive in, by a wide margin", "body": ["The Gothic Wars. Rome changes hands repeatedly between 536 and 552 and is besieged three times. In 537, during Vitiges’ siege, the Goths cut the aqueducts. The baths stopped, and so did the water-driven grain mills, which is why Belisarius moored floating mills in the current of the Tiber to keep bread being made at all. The Goths then tried to get in through the dry channel of the Aqua Virgo and were caught.", "The system never fully recovered. Popes repaired lines for centuries afterwards, but only the Aqua Virgo, low and mostly underground, ran continuously into the Middle Ages. After Totila took the city in 546, tradition holds Rome stood empty of inhabitants for about forty days: the only interruption in nearly three thousand years."], "danger": "Siege famine. Procopius, who was in the city, describes Romans eating nettles pulled from the ruins and officers profiteering on grain.", "ends": "Starvation, in weeks."},
  {"n": 7, "roman": "VII", "years": "601 – 700 CE", "regime": "pilgrim", "pop": [20000, 50000], "cluster": 0.35, "walls": "aurelian", "spill": 0, "popNote": "No count exists for this century or the next four. Historians’ guesses cluster between 20,000 and 50,000, inferred from inhabited area and churches in use.", "free": 999, "band": "lifetime", "title": "The city empties, and that is when it becomes survivable", "body": ["Rome is now a fraction of itself, scattered in patches across a walled area built for an imperial capital. Most of the enclosed thirteen square kilometres becomes the <i>disabitato</i>: vineyards, orchards, pasture, ruins and the occasional church. Not slums. Farmland, inside the walls.", "The city has also acquired an export and an import. The export is relics and absolution. The import is people who walk in with nothing. The bishop of Rome is now the effective government, and receiving arrivals is not a charitable sideline, it is the institution’s core business."], "danger": "Malaria in the low ground by the river, endemic and seasonal, which stays the city’s standing killer until the 20th century.", "ends": "Old age, if the summers miss you."},
  {"n": 8, "roman": "VIII", "years": "701 – 800 CE", "regime": "pilgrim", "pop": [20000, 50000], "cluster": 0.4, "walls": "aurelian", "spill": 0, "popNote": "Same absence of evidence. The population is inferred, not recorded.", "free": 999, "band": "lifetime", "title": "Rome builds a quarter for foreigners who cannot explain themselves", "body": ["The <i>Schola Saxonum</i>, the English quarter in the Borgo, is traditionally founded in 727 by Ine of Wessex, a king who abdicated in order to walk to Rome and die there. The date rests on later chroniclers, but the institution is real and it sat alongside Frankish, Frisian and Lombard <i>scholae</i>: national compounds with churches, burial grounds and lodging for arrivals who spoke no Latin.", "Read that against your own position. A man walks in, cannot speak, has no papers, knows nobody, and there is a building for him, run by people whose entire job is this. Not tolerated. Expected, and budgeted for. The district is still called Sassia, and in 1201 Innocent III turned the site into the Ospedale di Santo Spirito, which is a working hospital today."], "danger": "Lombard sieges: Aistulf besieges Rome in 756, Desiderius threatens it again in 772–73. The road in is more dangerous than the city.", "ends": "Old age."},
  {"n": 9, "roman": "IX", "years": "801 – 900 CE", "regime": "pilgrim", "pop": [20000, 40000], "cluster": 0.45, "walls": "leonine", "spill": 0, "popNote": "Inferred from the same thin material as the two centuries before it.", "free": 999, "band": "lifetime", "title": "Raided, walled again, still open to you", "body": ["In August 846 Arab raiders came up the Tiber and sacked St Peter’s and St Paul’s, which were vulnerable for one specific reason: both stood outside the Aurelian Walls. Leo IV answered by walling the Vatican. The Leonine City, finished around 852, is the first expansion of Rome’s defences in six hundred years, and it is the walled Borgo that every later pilgrim walks into.", "The city is poor, small and frequently ungoverned. None of that is your problem. Your problem was ever only whether there is a category here for a man who arrives on foot with nothing, and the category is the town’s entire economy."], "danger": "The 846 raid, and Arab bases on the Garigliano that made the pilgrim roads dangerous until 915.", "ends": "Old age."},
  {"n": 10, "roman": "X", "years": "901 – 1000 CE", "regime": "pilgrim", "pop": [20000, 40000], "cluster": 0.5, "walls": "leonine", "spill": 0, "popNote": "Inferred. Usually called the low point of the city’s institutions rather than of its population.", "free": 999, "band": "lifetime", "title": "Cattle in the Forum", "body": ["The centre of the ancient world is grazing land. The Forum is used as pasture and cattle market for so long that its ordinary working name becomes the <b>Campo Vaccino</b>, the cow field. The name is securely attested from the 16th century, Claude Lorrain painted it as pasture in 1636, and it was still a cow field when the first excavations began in 1803.", "The ground is rising as it goes. Demolition debris, Tiber silt and ordinary rubbish lift the low-lying centre by up to about nine metres over two thousand years. At the Pantheon you once climbed steps up to the porch; now you walk down into it."], "danger": "Baronial anarchy. Faction war, hostage-taking, mutilations. In 965 the city prefect was dragged through the streets by his hair. None of it is aimed at you.", "ends": "Old age."},
  {"n": 11, "roman": "XI", "years": "1001 – 1100 CE", "regime": "pilgrim", "pop": [20000, 40000], "cluster": 0.6, "walls": "leonine", "spill": 0, "popNote": "Inferred. The tight, dense abitato in the Tiber bend is a development of roughly this period, later than the older accounts assumed.", "free": 240, "band": "years", "title": "The Normans burn the part you live in", "body": ["On 28 May 1084 Robert Guiscard’s Normans took Rome to free Gregory VII from the emperor and burned a broad band of the inhabited city between the Caelian and the Colosseum. Captives were carried off and sold. Structurally it did more damage than either 5th-century sack.", "This is the century where the pilgrim regime is at its most dependable and the city itself is at its least. The institutions want you. The buildings are on fire."], "danger": "The Norman sack of 1084, and the tower-to-tower street warfare of the noble families that fills the rest of the century.", "ends": "Bad luck, in one specific decade."},
  {"n": 12, "roman": "XII", "years": "1101 – 1200 CE", "regime": "pilgrim", "pop": [20000, 40000], "cluster": 0.75, "walls": "leonine", "spill": 0, "popNote": "Inferred, and slowly rising.", "free": 999, "band": "lifetime", "title": "A commune, a guidebook, and a labour market", "body": ["Rome revives a commune in 1144, and by mid-century there is a written guide for visitors, the <i>Mirabilia Urbis Romae</i>, explaining the ruins to people who have walked several months to look at them. Pilgrimage now has a service sector: lodging, food, guiding, money-changing, portering, laundry, burial.", "That sector runs on unattached arrivals with strong backs. Here your lack of family, guild, letter or local claim is not a suspicious absence. It is the standard profile of the workforce."], "danger": "Malaria. In 1167 Frederick Barbarossa took the city and lost his army to Roman fever within weeks. The same fever is waiting for any summer arrival.", "ends": "Old age."},
  {"n": 13, "roman": "XIII", "years": "1201 – 1300 CE", "regime": "pilgrim", "pop": [30000, 35000], "cluster": 0.85, "walls": "leonine", "spill": 0, "popNote": "Around 30,000 residents by 1300 is the usual estimate, and still a reconstruction.", "free": 999, "band": "lifetime", "title": "The first Jubilee: the city invents the pilgrim crowd", "body": ["Boniface VIII proclaims a Jubilee in February 1300 and Rome is buried in people. Giovanni Villani, who was there, claims 200,000 pilgrims present at any one time through the year, which historians read as order-of-magnitude at best. Even discounted heavily, a town of thirty thousand absorbed several times itself.", "A city that does that has permanent machinery for strangers: hospices, brokers, bread, beds, and the shared assumption that an unexplained foreigner in the street is the normal condition of the place."], "danger": "Baronial tower warfare between the Orsini and the Colonna, and robbery of pilgrims among the fortified ruins.", "ends": "Old age."},
  {"n": 14, "roman": "XIV", "years": "1301 – 1400 CE", "regime": "pilgrim", "pop": [15000, 30000], "cluster": 0.9, "walls": "leonine", "spill": 0, "popNote": "When the papacy returned in 1377 the city held roughly 15,000 to 17,000 people. It is the lowest figure ever given for Rome and it is a reconstruction from hearths and parishes, not a census.", "free": 999, "band": "lifetime", "title": "The century that kills everyone else hires you", "body": ["The popes leave for Avignon in 1309 and Rome loses its only industry overnight. The Black Death arrives around August 1348, late, because a city with no pope had little traffic; estimates of mortality run to thirty or forty per cent, with no reliable count. In September 1349 an earthquake brings down the south flank of the Colosseum, and the rubble is quarried for the next three hundred years.", "You cannot catch it. You walk through the worst mortality event in European history untouched, into the aftermath, which is a labour shortage. That is the one condition on earth in which a man with no kin, no guild and no claim on anybody can name his price."], "danger": "Not the plague. A city with no pope, no government and no police: Cola di Rienzo, who briefly ruled it, was lynched on the Capitol on 8 October 1354.", "ends": "Old age, better off than you arrived."},
  {"n": 15, "roman": "XV", "years": "1401 – 1500 CE", "regime": "pilgrim", "pop": [25000, 55000], "cluster": 0.9, "walls": "leonine", "spill": 0, "popNote": "Recovering after the papacy returns for good in 1420. The <i>Descriptio Urbis</i> of 1526–27 finally counts about 54,000 people: the first real census of Rome since antiquity.", "free": 999, "band": "lifetime", "title": "The last century in which nobody asks", "body": ["The building sites open. Rome becomes a construction economy, quarrying its own ruins for lime and travertine, raising new churches and eventually a new St Peter’s, importing unskilled labour by the thousand.", "For an undocumented arrival this is the high point of the whole two thousand years: work that asks no questions, a hospice for every nation, charity as institutional routine, and the apparatus that will later demand to know who you are has not been built yet."], "danger": "Crowds. In the Jubilee of 1450 a panic on the Ponte Sant’Angelo crushed and drowned somewhere around 170 pilgrims.", "ends": "Old age."},
  {"n": 16, "roman": "XVI", "years": "1501 – 1600 CE", "regime": "papers", "pop": [10000, 100000], "cluster": 0.9, "walls": "leonine", "spill": 0.02, "popNote": "A violent swing: about 55,000 before 1527, perhaps 10,000 in the months after the Sack, then past 100,000 by 1600. The later figures are firmer precisely because this is the century Rome starts keeping registers.", "free": 60, "band": "years", "title": "The state learns to write people down", "body": ["On 6 May 1527 mutinous imperial troops storm the city and hold it for months. Six to ten thousand are killed; most of the population loss is flight, then plague and famine. That is the visible catastrophe.", "The invisible one matters more to you. The Roman Inquisition is re-established in 1542 and takes an active interest in foreigners. From the early 17th century every parish compiles a <i>stato delle anime</i>, a name-by-name census of every soul in every household, street by street, once a year at Easter. A hospice asks whether you are hungry. A register asks who you are, and writes down the answer."], "danger": "The Sack of 1527, and afterwards a countryside so full of bandits that Sixtus V displayed their heads in rows on the Ponte Sant’Angelo.", "ends": "A cell, or an expulsion order."},
  {"n": 17, "roman": "XVII", "years": "1601 – 1700 CE", "regime": "papers", "pop": [110000, 130000], "cluster": 0.85, "walls": "leonine", "spill": 0.03, "popNote": "Now genuinely counted, parish by parish, by the annual <i>stati delle anime</i>.", "free": 36, "band": "years", "title": "Baroque Rome, and a health pass at the gate", "body": ["The city is spectacular and it is administered. Every resident belongs to a parish and the parish knows their name. Having no fixed abode is a category with a punishment attached, and the great hospices are by now as much workhouse as refuge.", "Then the plague of 1656–57 arrives, and Rome answers it with quarantine, cordons and paperwork. A traveller reaching the gates without a <i>fede di sanità</i>, a health certificate, is not admitted. It is the first time in this city’s history that a piece of paper is the difference between inside and outside."], "danger": "The <i>sbirri</i>, the papal police, and confinement in the hospices for vagrancy.", "ends": "Institutionalised."},
  {"n": 18, "roman": "XVIII", "years": "1701 – 1800 CE", "regime": "papers", "pop": [135000, 165000], "cluster": 0.85, "walls": "leonine", "spill": 0.04, "popNote": "About 156,000 in 1750, from papal censuses. These are real numbers.", "free": 24, "band": "years", "title": "The Grand Tour arrives, with documents", "body": ["Rome fills with foreigners who cannot speak Italian, which sounds like your best chance since the 15th century. It is not. Those foreigners carry letters of introduction, bankers’ credit and travel papers, and the city has learned to sort the two kinds of stranger from each other at a glance.", "In February 1798 the French take Rome, proclaim a republic and deport the pope, who dies in France. Occupations are conducted with lists, and the Napoleonic administration is better at counting people than anything Rome has seen since the empire."], "danger": "Requisition, conscription and the census-taking that comes with a modern occupying army.", "ends": "Detained as a vagrant of no known nationality."},
  {"n": 19, "roman": "XIX", "years": "1801 – 1900 CE", "regime": "papers", "pop": [153000, 226000], "cluster": 0.8, "walls": "leonine", "spill": 0.12, "popNote": "About 153,000 in 1800; roughly 210,000 to 226,000 around the annexation of 1870–71, depending on whether you take the estimate or the first national census.", "free": 12, "band": "years", "title": "Everybody now has a nationality. You do not.", "body": ["Before unification you needed a passport simply to travel from Milan to Rome, between Italian states. Then the artillery breaches the Aurelian Walls at the Porta Pia on 20 September 1870, Rome becomes the capital of Italy, and the modern apparatus lands on it: the first national census in 1871, and in the same year a law making the <i>anagrafe</i>, the municipal population register, compulsory in every commune in the country.", "This is the trap in its final form. Every earlier Rome had a slot for a man who could not account for himself, whether that slot was a slave market or a pilgrim bed. This one has no slot at all. There is no country to return you to, because no country will admit that you exist."], "danger": "Malaria, still killing in the Campagna, and the Tiber, which had no embankments until the muraglioni were begun in 1876.", "ends": "A ward, an asylum, or the street."},
  {"n": 20, "roman": "XX", "years": "1901 – 2000 CE", "regime": "papers", "pop": [422000, 2805109], "cluster": 0.55, "walls": "leonine", "spill": 0.62, "popNote": "Counted properly. About 422,000 in 1901; 2,805,109 at the 1981 census, the highest the commune has ever recorded.", "free": 3, "band": "months", "title": "The richest Rome, and the one with no way in", "body": ["Antibiotics, surgery, clean water, hospitals that will treat anyone, and no chance at all. This city runs on documents: residence permits, work papers, ration cards, and from 1931 a compulsory national identity card. The city also finally bursts its 3rd-century wall and spreads across the Campagna.", "You would not starve here. Somebody would find you within days. Then a system with no category for you would spend the rest of your life deciding which of its wrong boxes to file you in."], "danger": "The German occupation, September 1943 to June 1944. On 16 October 1943, 1,259 people were seized in the ghetto and 1,023 deported; on 24 March 1944, 335 were shot at the Fosse Ardeatine. Papers were the difference between a life and a transport.", "ends": "Processed."}
];

